</div>
  <h2>Specification</h2>
  <p>Lights in Second Life are specified in LSL as linear red, green blue values in the range (0.0, 1.0). Internally light calculations are performed in the sRGB colorspace.</p>
  <p>The sRGB (Standard Red Green Blue) colorspace is defined in <a href="https://www.color.org/srgb04.xalter" target="_blank">IEC 61966-2-1:1999</a> and was developed as a joint project between Microsoft and HP.</p>
  <p>This calculation is known as an Inverse Electro-Optical Transfer Function (IEOTF).</p>
  <h2>Caveats</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>The name of this function is actually a misnomer as LSL color is actually in <a href="https://www.color.org/chardata/rgb/BT709.xalter" target="_blank">Rec.709</a> space, not <a href="https://www.color.org/chardata/rgb/srgb.xalter" target="_blank">sRGB</a> space. While they are very similar, the gamma value for sRGB is approx. 2.2, whereas the gamma value for Rec.709 is approx. 2.4.</li>
    <li>Scripting for PBR-enabled viewers: If you enter color vectors manually, using the viewer's color picker, enter them as sRGB values. The color picker automatically converts them internally to Linear RGB, so someone using a PBR-enabled viewer will see them correctly. When you apply color vectors directly with a script, however, you must apply sRGB to Blinn-Phong textured faces and Linear RGB to PBR materials.</li>
  </ul>
